> 
> 2.2.6 Extreme sea level mortality
> This category reflects the expectation of annual costs of mortality occurring due to ESL events, where death equivalents are valued using a value of a statistical life (VSL) framework, as employed in Diaz (2016), which assumes 1 % mortality for all populations exposed to a given ESL, based on Jonkman and Vrijling (2008). This is modeled similarly to the ESL-driven capital loss, except that the 1 % mortality assumption is used in place of the depth–damage function. In the implementation of Diaz (2016), both the mortality assumption and the depth–damage function appear to have been used in conjunction, although the text of the Diaz (2016) paper states that the depth–damage function should only be used in the estimation of capital stock damage, not mortality. We therefore corrected this discrepancy in our implementation of ESL-driven mortality estimates in pyCIAM.
